{
 "id": "tr-a1-questions",
 "cefr": "A1",
 "title": "Question formation",
 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what-is-it",
   "body": [
    {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Turkish has two question types. Yes/no questions are formed with the clitic "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mı / mi / mu / mü"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ", which is written as a separate word and must harmonise with the last vowel of the word before it. The clitic follows the focused word — usually the predicate — and carries the person ending in nominal and present-tense predicates (except 3rd-person plural -lAr, which stays before mI: geliyorlar mı?). In the past tense with "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "-dı/-di/-du/-dü"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ", the person ending stays on the verb and "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mı"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " is left bare. Content (WH) questions use question words such as "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "ne, nerede, kim, nasıl"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " in place of the unknown element; these words normally stand in the same position the answer word would occupy, rather than being moved to the front the way English WH-words are. When nothing else comes before it, the WH-word is simply first: Nerede oturuyorsun?"
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " Two more patterns build on mI. In an "
      },
      {
       "kind": "strong",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "either/or question"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " mI follows each option, and the options can be joined by "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "yoksa"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " ('or'): Otobüse mi biniyorsun, yoksa trene mi? And the invariable word "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "acaba"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " ('I wonder') can be added to any question, yes/no or WH, to make it sound tentative or curious."
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "title": "what is it?"
  },
  {
   "id": "when-to-use-it",
   "body": [
    {
     "kind": "list",
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Yes/no questions — nominal predicate."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " Use mI after a noun or adjective predicate when asking whether someone or something belongs to a category. The person ending moves from the predicate onto mI (3rd-person plural -lAr stays: öğrenciler mi?)."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Yes/no questions — present tense."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " Use mI after the present-tense verb stem + tense suffix. The person ending attaches to mI, not to the verb — except 3rd-person plural -lAr, which stays on the verb (geliyorlar mı?)."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Yes/no questions — past tense (-dı)."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " The person ending stays on the verb. mI follows the fully inflected verb and carries no extra ending."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Yes/no questions — existence (var/yok)."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " Place mI directly after "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "var"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " or "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "yok"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " to ask whether something exists or is available."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Content (WH) questions."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " Replace the unknown element with the appropriate question word. No mI clitic is used. The question word occupies the same slot as the answer would."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Focus questions — mI after another word."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " mI can also follow another word in the sentence (a person, a place, a time) instead of the verb. Then only that word is questioned, and the rest is taken for granted: "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Ankara'ya mı gidiyorsun?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " ('Is it to Ankara that you're going?'). The focused word normally sits just before the verb. Here mI stays bare: the person ending remains on the verb, in every tense."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Either/or (alternative) questions."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " To offer a choice, put mI after each option. "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "yoksa"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " ('or') between them is optional: "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Çay mı kahve mi istiyorsun?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " / "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Çay mı istiyorsun, yoksa kahve mi?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " The options can also be a predicate and its negative, to mean 'or not': "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Geliyor musun, gelmiyor musun?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ", "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Var mı, yok mu?"
           }
          ]
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "acaba — 'I wonder'."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " Add "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "acaba"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " to a yes/no or WH question to show doubt or curiosity. It usually stands at the start or the end of the sentence. It never changes form, and it does not replace mI: a yes/no question keeps its mI ("
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Acaba otobüs geç mi?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "), and a WH question still has none ("
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Ali nerede acaba?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ")."
         }
        ]
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ],
   "title": "when to use it"
  },
  {
   "id": "formation",
   "body": [
    {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "The form of mI is determined by four-way vowel harmony based on the last vowel of the preceding word."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "head": [
      "last vowel in preceding word",
      "clitic form"
     ],
     "kind": "conjugation-table",
     "rows": [
      [
       "a, ı",
       "mı"
      ],
      [
       "e, i",
       "mi"
      ],
      [
       "o, u",
       "mu"
      ],
      [
       "ö, ü",
       "mü"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "For yes/no questions, the person endings that normally attach to the predicate are instead attached to mI; only 3rd-person plural -lAr stays on the predicate. Because mI ends in a vowel, a buffer "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "-y-"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " comes before the vowel-initial endings -Im and -Iz: mi-y-im, mu-y-uz. The table below shows the pattern for a nominal predicate and for the present-continuous tense."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "head": [
      "person",
      "nominal predicate (öğrenci — student)",
      "present continuous (geliyor — is coming)"
     ],
     "kind": "conjugation-table",
     "rows": [
      [
       "ben (I)",
       "öğrenci miyim?",
       "geliyor muyum?"
      ],
      [
       "sen (you, sg.)",
       "öğrenci misin?",
       "geliyor musun?"
      ],
      [
       "o (he/she/it)",
       "öğrenci mi?",
       "geliyor mu?"
      ],
      [
       "biz (we)",
       "öğrenci miyiz?",
       "geliyor muyuz?"
      ],
      [
       "siz (you, pl.)",
       "öğrenci misiniz?",
       "geliyor musunuz?"
      ],
      [
       "onlar (they)",
       "öğrenciler mi?",
       "geliyorlar mı?"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "In the "
      },
      {
       "kind": "strong",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "-dı past"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ", the person ending remains on the verb. mI follows as a bare clitic with no ending."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "head": [
      "person",
      "-dı past question (git — to go)"
     ],
     "kind": "conjugation-table",
     "rows": [
      [
       "ben",
       "gittim mi?"
      ],
      [
       "sen",
       "gittin mi?"
      ],
      [
       "o",
       "gitti mi?"
      ],
      [
       "biz",
       "gittik mi?"
      ],
      [
       "siz",
       "gittiniz mi?"
      ],
      [
       "onlar",
       "gittiler mi?"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Common WH-words at A1. The answer slot is taken by the WH-word, so the verb keeps its normal person ending and no mI is added. "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "nere-"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " ('where') normally carries a case ending: nerede (at), nereye (to), nereden (from)."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "conjugation-table",
     "head": [
      "question word",
      "meaning",
      "example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "ne",
       "what",
       "Bu ne?"
      ],
      [
       "kim",
       "who",
       "Bu kim?"
      ],
      [
       "nerede",
       "where (at)",
       "Ali nerede?"
      ],
      [
       "nereye",
       "where (to)",
       "Nereye gidiyorsun?"
      ],
      [
       "nereden",
       "where (from)",
       "Nereden geliyorsun?"
      ],
      [
       "ne zaman",
       "when",
       "Ne zaman geliyorsun?"
      ],
      [
       "nasıl",
       "how",
       "Bugün hava nasıl?"
      ],
      [
       "neden / niye / niçin",
       "why",
       "Neden gülüyorsun?"
      ],
      [
       "hangi",
       "which",
       "Hangi kitabı okuyorsun?"
      ],
      [
       "kaç",
       "how many (+ singular noun, often with tane); what time (saat kaç)",
       "Kaç kardeşin var?"
      ],
      [
       "ne kadar",
       "how much",
       "Bu çanta ne kadar?"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Where mI goes decides where the person ending goes. When mI follows the predicate, the ending moves onto mI (also in other tenses you will meet later: gelir misin?, gelecek misin?), except in the -dı past and with 3rd-person plural -lAr. When mI follows any other word (a focused word, or an option in an either/or question), mI is bare and the verb keeps its ending. mI always harmonises with the word right before it."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "conjugation-table",
     "head": [
      "mI follows…",
      "example",
      "person ending"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "the predicate (present)",
       "Ankara'ya gidiyor musun?",
       "on mI (mu-sun)"
      ],
      [
       "the predicate (-dı past)",
       "Ankara'ya gittin mi?",
       "on the verb (gitti-n)"
      ],
      [
       "a focused word (a → mı)",
       "Ankara'ya mı gidiyorsun?",
       "on the verb (gidiyor-sun)"
      ],
      [
       "a focused word (e → mi)",
       "Otobüse mi biniyorsun?",
       "on the verb"
      ],
      [
       "a focused word (u → mu)",
       "Okulu mu seviyorsun?",
       "on the verb"
      ],
      [
       "a focused word (ü → mü)",
       "Bugün mü geliyorsun?",
       "on the verb"
      ],
      [
       "a focused subject (past)",
       "Sen mi yaptın?",
       "on the verb (yaptı-n)"
      ],
      [
       "each option (either/or)",
       "Ankara'ya mı İstanbul'a mı gidiyorsun?",
       "on the verb"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "paragraph",
     "text": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "In an either/or question, the shared verb is usually said once: after the first option ("
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "Otobüse mi biniyorsun, yoksa trene mi?"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ") or after the last one ("
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "Otobüse mi trene mi biniyorsun?"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "). When the options are the predicate itself, each one is a complete yes/no question and follows the normal rules: "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "Geliyor musun, gelmiyor musun?"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " (ending on mI), "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "Geldin mi, gelmedin mi?"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " (ending on the verb)."
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "title": "formation"
  },
  {
   "id": "examples-in-context",
   "body": [
    {
     "en": "Are you coming?",
     "kind": "example",
     "note": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Present continuous yes/no question. The last vowel of "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "geliyor"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " is o, so the clitic is "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mu"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "; the 2sg ending -sun attaches to it."
      }
     ],
     "target": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Geliyor "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "musun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "?"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "en": "Are you a student?",
     "kind": "example",
     "note": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Nominal predicate question. The person ending -sin moves off the noun and onto "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mi"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ". Saying "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "öğrencisin mi"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " is a common learner error."
      }
     ],
     "target": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Sen öğrenci "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "misin"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "?"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "en": "Did you go to the cinema yesterday?",
     "kind": "example",
     "note": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Past tense question. The -n person ending stays on the verb "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "gittin"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "; "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mi"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " is bare."
      }
     ],
     "target": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Dün sinemaya gittin "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mi"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "?"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "en": "Is there milk at home?",
     "kind": "example",
     "note": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Existence question with "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "var"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ". The last vowel of "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "var"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " is a, so the clitic is "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mı"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": ". No person ending is added."
      }
     ],
     "target": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Evde süt var "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "mı"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "?"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "en": "What is this?",
     "kind": "example",
     "note": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Content question. "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "Ne"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": " replaces the predicate; no mI clitic is used."
      }
     ],
     "target": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Bu "
      },
      {
       "kind": "hilite",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "ne"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "?"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "en": "Where do you live?",
     "kind": "example",
     "note": [
      {
       "kind": "text",
       "text": "Content question. "
      },
      {
       "kind": "em",
       "children": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "Nerede"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "kind": "text",
