   "title": "examples in context"
  },
  {
   "id": "common-pitfalls",
   "body": [
    {
     "kind": "list",
     "items": [
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Keeping the person ending on the predicate."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " In nominal and present-tense yes/no questions the person ending must move to mI. "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "öğrencisin mi"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " is wrong; the correct form is "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "öğrenci misin"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Wrong vowel harmony on mI."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " The clitic must match the last vowel of the preceding word. After "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "geliyor"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " (last vowel o) the clitic is "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "mu"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ", not "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "mi"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ". Check the last vowel every time."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Fronting WH-words English-style."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " Turkish does not need to move question words to the front: they stand where the answer would stand, usually right before the verb. Say "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Ahmet nerede oturuyor?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " (Where does Ahmet live?), just like the answer "
         },
         {
          "kind": "em",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Ahmet İstanbul'da oturuyor."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " When nothing else comes before it, the WH-word is simply first, as in "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Nerede oturuyorsun?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Applying the present-tense pattern to the -dı past."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " In the past tense the person ending stays on the verb. "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "geldi misin"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " is wrong; the correct form is "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "geldin mi"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ". The mI clitic in past-tense questions carries no person ending."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Putting the person ending on a focus or either/or mI."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " When mI follows a word other than the predicate, it takes no ending. "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Ankara'ya mısın gidiyor?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " is wrong; the correct form is "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Ankara'ya mı gidiyorsun?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Dropping the second mI in an either/or question."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " Every option needs its own mI. "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Otobüse mi biniyorsun, yoksa tren?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " is wrong; say "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Otobüse mi biniyorsun, yoksa trene mi?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "."
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "text": [
         {
          "kind": "strong",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Leaving out mI after acaba."
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " acaba adds 'I wonder', but in standard Turkish a yes/no question with acaba still takes mI. Write "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Acaba otobüs geç mi?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": ", not "
         },
         {
          "kind": "hilite",
          "children": [
           {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Acaba otobüs geç?"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": " acaba itself never changes form."
         }
        ]
       }
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "callout",
     "variant": "warn",
     "children": [
      {
       "kind": "paragraph",
       "text": [
        {
         "kind": "text",
         "text": "A quick rule of thumb: when mI follows the predicate, the person ending goes on mI (öğrenci misin?, geliyor musun?), except in the -dı past (gittin mi?) and with 3rd-person plural -lAr, which comes before mI (geliyorlar mı?). When mI follows any other word, the verb keeps its own ending and mI is bare (Ankara'ya mı gidiyorsun?)."
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "title": "common pitfalls"
  }
 ],
 "subtitle": "mı, WH-words, either/or questions and acaba"
}